---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyFigure from "../../components/CaseStudyFigure.astro";
import LinkButton from "../../components/LinkButton.astro";
import ObjectDensityDemo from "../../components/ObjectDensityDemo.astro";

import boardImg from "../../assets/images/tive-board.webp";
import reviewPrepImg from "../../assets/images/tive-review-prep.png";
import validationMethodsImg from "../../assets/images/tive-validation-methods.png";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");

const sections = [
  { id: "challenge", label: "Challenge" },
  { id: "outcomes", label: "Results" },
  { id: "team", label: "Team" },
  { id: "manager", label: "Manager" },
  { id: "judgment", label: "Judgment" },
  { id: "people", label: "People" },
  { id: "learned", label: "Learned" },
  { id: "product", label: "Also" },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Scaling Design Judgment · Brian Cleveland"
  navLabel="Leadership at Tive Case Study"
  sections={sections}
>
  <!-- HERO + AT A GLANCE -->
  <header class="content-section">
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Design leadership at Tive • 3rd Quarter 2026</p>
    <h1 class="heading-1">Scaling design judgment, not just design output</h1>
    <p class="cs-lede">
      Evolving a senior product design team from reactive execution toward an autonomous, strategic function.
    </p>
    <p class="body-3 soft">Tive is a global supply chain visibility platform, growing rapidly as a Series C company.</p>
    <ul class="cs-stats">
      <li><span class="value word">Web · Mobile · Hardware</span><span class="label">Cross-platform design leadership</span></li>
      <li><span class="value">4</span><span class="label">Key product areas</span></li>
      <li><span class="value word">Player / coach</span><span class="label">Management + hands-on systems work</span></li>
      <li><span class="value word">1 hr → 10 min</span><span class="label">Prep time per design review, self-timed</span></li>
    </ul>
    <dl class="cs-facts rows">
      <div>
        <dt>Context</dt>
        <dd>Enterprise software spanning web, native mobile, and connected hardware, while the engineering team was growing quickly and adopting AI.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>My role</dt>
        <dd>Product Design Manager and player-coach, partnering with product managers, engineering managers, and the leadership team.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The problem</dt>
        <dd>Product and engineering were increasingly &ldquo;waiting on design.&rdquo; A stretched senior team needed a repeatable way to focus on strategic work and let others make good design decisions.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>What I changed</dt>
        <dd>A lightweight operating model for visibility and ownership, an AI-assisted review-prep workflow, and a risk-based framework for how much evidence each decision needs.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>What happened</dt>
        <dd>We <i>rarely</i> hear &ldquo;waiting on design&rdquo; anymore. Designers moved from reactive product partners to proactive design leaders, driving architectural changes like tables and global navigation redesigns, and design review moved from UI critique and context sharing to systemic change and architecture evaluation.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </header>

  <!-- THE LEADERSHIP CHALLENGE -->
  <section class="content-section" id="challenge">
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">The leadership challenge</p>
    <h2 class="heading-3">&ldquo;We're waiting on design.&rdquo;<br />No, you're waiting on design mocks.</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>That feedback got louder as the engineering team grew and adopted AI to speed through code. We were launching faster, with less design confidence and more pressure on the team, and we were stretched thin <b>trying to do it all</b>.</p>
      <p>We were spending our time on tedious, tactical work, and we had no repeatable framework for big-rock design work or for consistently designing at a high level. Engineers had come to expect every flow and edge-case to have a high-fidelity Figma mock.</p>
      <p>We needed to be less reactive and more proactive to keep a high bar for UX while reducing design debt.</p>
      <p>The goal wasn't to add process for process&rsquo;s sake or to be a decision gatekeeper; it was to create <i>enough</i> structure and context that designers could consistently operate more <b>independently</b> while raising the <b>quality and consistency</b> of the work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-compare">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-flow muted" role="group" aria-label="Before (the waterfall): a linear hand-off">
        <p class="cs-compare-label">Before (the waterfall)</p>
        <div class="cs-node">Product requirements</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
        <div class="cs-node">Design</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
        <div class="cs-node">Mocks</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
        <div class="cs-node">Engineering</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-panel cs-flow" role="group" aria-label="After (moving forward together): shared context and designer ownership">
        <p class="cs-compare-label accent">After (moving forward together)</p>
        <div class="cs-node">Product strategy</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
        <div class="cs-triad"><div class="cs-node">Product</div><i>↔</i><div class="cs-node strong">Design</div><i>↔</i><div class="cs-node">Eng</div></div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
        <div class="cs-node">Shared context</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
        <div class="cs-node accent">Designer ownership</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
        <div class="cs-node">Evidence + critique</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
        <div class="cs-node">Execution</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="cs-callout">The question became: <em>how do I scale design judgment without becoming the bottleneck?</em></p>
  </section>

  <!-- RESULTS -->
  <section class="content-section" id="outcomes">
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Results</p>
    <h2 class="heading-3">What changed</h2>
    <ul class="cs-results">
      <li>
        <span class="kicker">Team</span>
        <span class="value word">More ownership</span>
        <p>Designers engaging earlier, driving alignment, and operating with greater autonomy.</p>
        <p class="proof">Designers lead large architectural changes freely and with clearer direction.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="kicker">Quality</span>
        <span class="value word">Better judgment</span>
        <p>More informed critique, intentional validation, and more attention to shared patterns.</p>
        <p class="proof">Mobile app architecture is now scaled strategically instead of by hunch. Time to launch decreased noticeably and launches are of a higher quality, resulting in fewer &ldquo;fast-follows.&rdquo;</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="kicker">Leverage</span>
        <span class="value word">Less coordination overhead</span>
        <p>AI and lightweight operations make context, feedback, and cross-team awareness easier to maintain.</p>
        <p class="proof">By my own timing, prepping for design critique dropped from about an hour to about ten minutes across roughly 20 reviews.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <blockquote class="cs-quote">
      &ldquo;Design is finally involved in product roadmap discussions and it&rsquo;s a great thing!&rdquo;
      <cite>Senior product designer</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW I DID IT -->
  <section class="content-section" id="model">
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">How I did it</p>
    <h2 class="heading-3">Three places where better systems created leverage.</h2>
    <div class="cs-cols links">
      <a href="#team">
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">01 · TEAM</p>
        <h3>Scale the team</h3>
        <p>Create autonomy without losing alignment.</p>
        <span class="go">Operating model ↓</span>
      </a>
      <a href="#manager">
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">02 · MANAGER</p>
        <h3>Scale the manager</h3>
        <p>Use AI to raise the quality of feedback.</p>
        <span class="go">Review prep ↓</span>
      </a>
      <a href="#judgment">
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">03 · JUDGMENT</p>
        <h3>Scale judgment</h3>
        <p>Match design rigor to actual product risk.</p>
        <span class="go">Risk framework ↓</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 TEAM -->
  <section class="content-section" id="team">
    <div class="cs-decision-head">
      <span class="index" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Scale the team</p>
        <h2 class="heading-3">Autonomy requires <i>visibility</i>, not more <i>approval gates</i>.</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-split">
      <div class="cs-prose">
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Problem</p>
        <p>Designers were spending long hours on mocks when what we needed was <b>bigger, systemic changes</b>.</p>
        <p>In addition to other forums for design alignment, I introduced Design Triage: a dedicated space to align on our shared work and pull in design system and architectural big-rocks alongside our feature work.</p>
        <CaseStudyFigure
          src={boardImg}
          alt="The design team's board with five columns, Needs Triage, Want to Do, Designing, In Review or Blocked, and Design Done, holding cards such as Table Migration, Card Migration, and Button component micro interactions."
          caption="The <b>DS / patterns</b> board, live: design-system work moves from Needs Triage through Designing to Design Done."
        />
      </div>
      <div class="cs-panel">
        <div class="cs-ops" role="group" aria-label="Team operating model">
          <div class="cs-node">Upcoming work</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
          <div class="cs-node strong">Design triage</div>
          <div class="cs-lanes">
            <div class="cs-node">Product initiatives</div>
            <div class="cs-node linked">DS / patterns</div>
            <div class="cs-node">UX papercuts</div>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-join"></div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
          <div class="cs-node accent">Designer ownership</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
          <div class="cs-node">Jam · critique · alignment</div><div class="cs-arrow"></div>
          <div class="cs-node">Development</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-cols">
      <div><h3>Design Triage</h3><p>Weekly visibility into upcoming work, conflicts, risks, and opportunities.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Async coordination</h3><p>Less meeting overhead; better awareness of related work, AI-assistance where beneficial.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Design Jam</h3><p>Earlier collaboration, including PMs. Documented decisions and shared notes.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Product-design-at-Tive Slack Channel</h3><p>Quicker ideas from anyone into tickets.</p></div>
    </div>
    <aside class="cs-takeaway">
      <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Result</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Designers can now drive bigger architectural changes in support of the product roadmap.</li>
        <li>I now had visibility into the decisions where my involvement had the most impact.</li>
      </ol>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 MANAGER -->
  <section class="content-section" id="manager">
    <div class="cs-decision-head">
      <span class="index" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Scale the manager</p>
        <h2 class="heading-3">AI helped me spend less time reconstructing context and more time challenging the work.</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-split">
      <div class="cs-prose">
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Problem</p>
        <p>Managing senior designers across <i>many</i> simultaneous initiatives created a problem: <i>high quality feedback required gathering a lot of context before every review.</i></p>
        <p>So I built a review-prep workflow in Claude. Give it a project name, it gathers recent context across Slack, Google Drive, and Figma and structures it around the questions I need answered before a critique.</p>
        <p>In a few minutes I can see the problem statement, the decisions made during exploration, the tradeoffs and engineering input, and the open questions. It&rsquo;s like sitting in the planning meetings for every major project. I stop asking designers to replay all of the decisions made, and spend critique on strategy (&ldquo;Did you consider the work designer B is doing on product X?&rdquo; &ldquo;What persona are you targeting with option C?&rdquo;) instead of pixel-level feedback.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-panel">
        <div class="cs-prep" role="group" aria-label="AI design review prep workflow">
          <div class="cs-sources"><div class="cs-node">Slack</div><div class="cs-node">Drive</div><div class="cs-node">Figma</div></div>
          <div class="cs-wire"></div>
          <div class="cs-node strong">Claude<br />review prep</div>
          <div class="cs-wire"></div>
          <div class="cs-qs">
            <div class="cs-node">Why this solution?</div>
            <div class="cs-node">Alternatives explored?</div>
            <div class="cs-node">Tradeoffs?</div>
            <div class="cs-node">Open questions?</div>
            <div class="cs-node accent out">→ Design critique</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <CaseStudyFigure
      src={reviewPrepImg}
      alt="Excerpt from an AI-generated design review prep brief: a Biggest unanswered questions section listing open decisions about scope, architecture, edge cases, carried-over scope, and QA ownership."
      caption="From a review-prep brief: the open questions worth challenging, gathered before the critique starts."
    />
    <aside class="cs-takeaway">
      <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Result</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Faster context and fewer basic questions, so reviews go straight to better feedback. By my own timing, prep dropped from about an hour to about ten minutes across roughly 20 reviews.</li>
        <li>AI didn't replace design judgment. It created more room for it.</li>
      </ol>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 JUDGMENT -->
  <section class="content-section" id="judgment">
    <div class="cs-decision-head">
      <span class="index" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Scale judgment</p>
        <h2 class="heading-3">Not every design problem deserves the same process.</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-split">
      <div class="cs-prose">
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">System</p>
        <p>Four parts that work together.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Risk scoring:</b> Rate each decision by impact and uncertainty.</li>
          <li><b>Validation library:</b> Pick the right method, from heuristic review and analytics to live in-product validation, surveys, interviews, and usability testing.</li>
          <li><b>Design Triage:</b> Talk through risk calls as a team.</li>
          <li><b>AI analysis tool:</b> Open to the broader org, not just designers.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-panel">
        <div class="cs-matrix" role="table" aria-label="Validation by impact and uncertainty">
          <div class="ax" role="columnheader"></div>
          <div class="ax col" role="columnheader">Low uncertainty</div>
          <div class="ax col" role="columnheader">High uncertainty</div>
          <div class="ax" role="rowheader">Low impact</div>
          <div class="cell c1" role="cell"><small>Evidence: light</small><b>Ship + observe</b></div>
          <div class="cell c2" role="cell"><small>Evidence: some</small><b>Some lightweight validation</b></div>
          <div class="ax" role="rowheader">High impact</div>
          <div class="cell c3" role="cell"><small>Evidence: focused</small><b>Focused, targeted validation</b></div>
          <div class="cell c4" role="cell"><small>Evidence: deep</small><b>Deep research + deeper validation</b></div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-pipeline">
          <span class="cs-node">Assess risk</span><i>→</i><span class="cs-node">Choose method</span><i>→</i><span class="cs-node">Gather evidence</span><i>→</i><span class="cs-node accent">Decide</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <CaseStudyFigure
      src={validationMethodsImg}
      alt="Excerpt from the Design Operations Manual listing validation methods: heuristic or expert review, analytics and behavioral review, Pendo live validation, survey, and user interview, each with what it answers, when to use it, and examples."
      caption="Validation method examples as listed in the Design Operations Manual"
      contain
    />
    <aside class="cs-takeaway">
      <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Result</p>
      <p>App architecture decisions are now made strategically rather than by hunch. Time to launch dropped from two or more sprints to one for many initiatives, and launches are higher quality.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <!-- THE PEOPLE SIDE + FAILED EXPERIMENT -->
  <section class="content-section" id="people">
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">The people side</p>
    <h2 class="heading-3">A system only works if the designers grow with it.</h2>
    <div class="cs-cols">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Ownership</p>
        <h3>Bigger scope</h3>
        <p>One designer led the tables redesign end to end, including PM and engineering alignment. They took ownership of systems work beyond a typical product scope and made systemic architectural changes inside product feature work.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Coaching</p>
        <h3>How I coach</h3>
        <p>Senior designers need space to do their best work. I meet with them 1:1 every week, focus on growing systems-level and holistic solutions over granular pixel-pushing, and keep improving our shared processes so designers can spend their time on the most valuable work.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Team</p>
        <h3>Hiring and growth</h3>
        <p>I hired versatile, senior designers and created individual growth frameworks and a design team leveling rubric.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="failed">
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">One experiment that failed</p>
    <h2 class="heading-3">Scaling also meant knowing when to remove process.</h2>
    <div class="cs-cols">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Experiment</p>
        <h3>Design Brief v1</h3>
        <p>A running artifact meant to preserve design goals and decisions throughout an initiative.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">What happened</p>
        <ul class="cs-xlist">
          <li>Hard to maintain</li>
          <li>Poor discoverability</li>
          <li>Didn't solve a big enough problem</li>
          <li>Added process when it wasn't needed</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Decision</p>
        <span class="cs-stamp">No Go</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Going Forward</p>
        <p>Closer PM-designer-EM collaboration and project-specific shared documents served us better.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHAT I LEARNED -->
  <section class="content-section" id="learned">
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">What I learned</p>
    <h2 class="heading-3">The manager shouldn't be <i>the system.</i></h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>The biggest shift in my management approach was realizing that scaling a senior team isn't about getting better at keeping track of everything, everywhere. It's about building systems that <b>empower the team to do their most valuable work</b>.</p>
      <p>Clear ownership creates autonomy. Shared frameworks build consistency and free up valuable time. AI reduces the cost of context. And good design architecture lets individual decisions compound into something larger.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ALSO: SCALING THE PRODUCT -->
  <section class="content-section" id="product">
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Also: scaling the product</p>
    <h2 class="heading-3">Some design problems shouldn't be solved one feature at a time.</h2>
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Observation</p>
    <div class="cs-split even">
      <div class="cs-prose"><p>We kept displaying the same underlying product objects in very different contexts. Solving each instance independently created inconsistency and lengthy, repeated design work.</p></div>
      <div class="cs-prose"><p>So we defined each core object once—with shared hierarchy and interaction rules—and let context set the density. <b>Carrier and Driver</b> were the first to be trialed in Pickup Verification and Commodities work.</p></div>
    </div>
    <ObjectDensityDemo />
    <div class="cs-cols">
      <div><p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Consistency</p><p>The same objects behave predictably everywhere.</p></div>
      <div><p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Velocity</p><p>Designers stop reinventing common presentation problems.</p></div>
      <div><p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">Scale</p><p>New objects inherit an established framework.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">The platform layer underneath</p>
    <div class="cs-cols">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">FIGMA ↔ CODE</p>
        <h3>Library audits against the codebase</h3>
        <p>Audited Figma libraries against code; pushed for foundations and components as separate published files, variable names that match code tokens literally, and Code Connect to close the interpretation gap.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">WEB ↔ MOBILE</p>
        <h3>A boundary for the platform</h3>
        <p>Sorted features into Configure / Monitor / Act / Investigate. Configuration lives on web; lightweight action lives on mobile — a rule for what to standardize and what to differentiate.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow all-caps soft">AI ↔ SYSTEM</p>
        <h3>AI-assisted audits &amp; handoff</h3>
        <p>A reusable AI workflow for auditing component libraries and generating developer handoff documentation — making the system cheaper to keep honest.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="content-section">
    <div class="cs-sign">
      <span>Brian Cleveland · Design leadership case study</span>
      <span>Internal details generalized for confidentiality</span>
    </div>
    <nav class="navigation-list" aria-label="More case studies">
      <ul>
        <li><LinkButton href={`${base}/#my-work`} label="Back to all work" /></li>
        <li><LinkButton href={`${base}/work/global-navigation`} label="Read: One navigation system, globally adopted" inverse /></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style is:global>
  /* The DS / patterns node lights up while the board screenshot is hovered. */
  #team .cs-split:has(.cs-zoom:hover) .cs-node.linked {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-vibrant) 30%, var(--color-bg));
  }
</style>
